          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What makes a cassette panel different from a regular flat panel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Cassette panels have folded or returned edges that allow them to clip onto a substructure using concealed fixing, giving a seamless appearance without visible screws or rivets, unlike face-fixed flat panels that show their fasteners."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a single damaged cassette panel be replaced easily?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, this is one of the system's key practical advantages. Since each panel clips independently onto the substructure, a damaged panel can typically be removed and replaced without disturbing surrounding panels or the rest of the elevation."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What material are cassette panels usually made from?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Aluminium is the standard choice, valued for its light weight and corrosion resistance, which suits the folding and clipping process cassette panels rely on and keeps the load on the substructure manageable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cassette panels more expensive than standard flat cladding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally yes, since the folded edge profile requires additional fabrication, though cassette panels remain considerably more economical than complex parametric or perforated systems built from many unique panel shapes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why do cassette panel installations need such precise substructure alignment?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because cassette joints are uniform and precise by design, any inconsistency in the substructure spacing or level shows up as visible misalignment in the finished facade more noticeably than with less standardised panel systems."
          }
        }
      ]
